WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.601
Look around this room. Every wall is a
rectangle, and every corner is an angle.

2
00:00:09.821 --> 00:00:16.973
Today you will take solids apart, fold flat
shapes back up, and measure every shape you find.

3
00:00:17.473 --> 00:00:25.461
Every solid is built from flat faces, the edges where
two faces meet, and the vertices where edges meet.

4
00:00:25.681 --> 00:00:31.346
A cube. Six faces, all identical squares.
Twelve edges, and eight vertices.

5
00:00:31.566 --> 00:00:40.065
A square-based pyramid. One square face on the bottom, and
four triangles leaning up to a point. Five faces in all.

6
00:00:40.285 --> 00:00:48.783
A cylinder is different. Two flat circles, top and bottom,
joined by one curved surface that is not flat at all.

7
00:00:49.283 --> 00:00:57.039
A net is a solid opened out flat. Fold every
edge, and it closes back up into the solid again.

8
00:00:57.259 --> 00:01:03.575
Six squares in a cross fold up into a cube.
Every solid has more than one net that works.

9
00:01:03.795 --> 00:01:15.219
But not every six squares will do. Six in a straight line only roll up into a tube,
with nothing left over to make the top or the bottom. That is not a net of a cube.

10
00:01:15.719 --> 00:01:23.242
A line of symmetry is a fold that makes both halves match
exactly. Find all of them, not just the first one you spot.

11
00:01:23.462 --> 00:01:28.153
A square has four: up and down,
side to side, and both diagonals.

12
00:01:28.373 --> 00:01:31.484
This triangle has only one,
straight up the middle.

13
00:01:31.704 --> 00:01:39.877
And a parallelogram, even though it looks even, has
none at all. No fold ever makes its halves match.

14
00:01:40.377 --> 00:01:49.944
A reflection is what you see in a mirror. Every square goes straight
across the mirror line, and lands the same distance away on the other side.

15
00:01:50.164 --> 00:02:00.985
This shape is two squares from the mirror line, so its reflection is two squares
from the line on the other side. And look: it is flipped, facing the other way.

16
00:02:01.205 --> 00:02:11.328
When the mirror line is the edge of the shape, the reflection touches it. The
shape and its reflection meet along the line, like the two wings of a butterfly.

17
00:02:11.548 --> 00:02:20.093
A horizontal mirror line works in just the same way. Every
square goes straight down, the same distance on the other side.

18
00:02:20.593 --> 00:02:28.302
An angle is the amount of turn between two lines. A
right angle is a square corner, exactly ninety degrees.

19
00:02:28.522 --> 00:02:31.123
Smaller than a right angle is acute.

20
00:02:31.343 --> 00:02:35.941
Bigger than a right angle, but still
less than a straight line, is obtuse.

21
00:02:36.161 --> 00:02:43.452
To estimate an angle, compare it with a right angle.
This one is about half a right angle, so it is acute.

22
00:02:43.672 --> 00:02:54.399
Now compare these two. Angle A has the longer lines, but angle B is the bigger
angle, because an angle is the amount of turn, not the length of its lines.

23
00:02:54.899 --> 00:03:08.924
Put two identical right-angled triangles together along their longest side, and they make a
rectangle. When their two shorter sides are the same length, as these are, that rectangle is a square.

24
00:03:09.144 --> 00:03:24.376
Six equilateral triangles, with every side the same length, fit together round a point to make
a regular hexagon. And hexagons tile a floor with no gaps and no overlaps. That is tessellation.

25
00:03:24.596 --> 00:03:30.355
Circles never tessellate. Pack them however
you like, there are always gaps left over.

26
00:03:30.855 --> 00:03:37.867
Area is how many squares cover a shape. Count them
one at a time, and this rectangle is fifteen squares.

27
00:03:38.087 --> 00:03:53.227
Now look at the rows. There are five squares in each row, and three rows. Five times three is fifteen,
the same as counting them all. That is why the area of a rectangle is its length times its width.

28
00:03:53.727 --> 00:04:00.182
Perimeter is different. It is the distance all
the way round the outside, not the space inside.

29
00:04:00.402 --> 00:04:10.944
Hodan puts a fence around a garden six metres long and four metres
wide. Six, add four, add six, add four: twenty metres of fence.

30
00:04:11.164 --> 00:04:22.774
A rectangle has two lengths and two widths, so there is a short way too. Add the
length and the width, then double it. Six and four make ten, and double ten is twenty.

31
00:04:23.274 --> 00:04:31.308
Some shapes are not one rectangle. Split an L shape
into two rectangles you already know how to measure.

32
00:04:31.528 --> 00:04:41.048
A five by three rectangle is fifteen squares. A two by two
square is four more. Add the areas: nineteen squares in all.

33
00:04:41.548 --> 00:04:49.071
A leaf is not a rectangle at all, so lay it on a grid
instead. Count every square that is completely inside.

34
00:04:49.291 --> 00:05:03.038
Nine whole squares. Twelve more are only partly covered, so count each of those as
about a half: six more. About fifteen squares in all - an estimate, not an exact count.

35
00:05:03.538 --> 00:05:12.315
A measuring jug is marked every hundred millilitres, with three
small marks between each pair, splitting the gap into quarters.

36
00:05:12.535 --> 00:05:23.123
The water sits three quarters of the way past two hundred. Three quarters of a
hundred is seventy five, so it reads two hundred and seventy five millilitres.

37
00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:36.626
Solids and their nets. Every line of symmetry a shape has. Acute,
right and obtuse. Shapes that tessellate, and shapes that leave gaps.

38
00:05:36.846 --> 00:05:46.320
Area by counting or by the formula, perimeter all the way round,
an estimate on a grid, and a scale read between the marks.
